<div class="takeaways"><h2>Key Takeaways</h2><ul>
  <li>A doctorate in sports psychology is not one degree but two paths: a licensure-track psychology doctorate with a sport emphasis, or an applied and research doctorate in kinesiology or sport and exercise psychology.</li>
  <li>No APA-accredited PhD in sports psychology exists, because the APA does not accredit that specialty. The only APA-accredited program among the seven on this page is a counseling psychology doctorate at the University of North Texas.</li>
  <li>A PhD typically takes five to seven years; funded research PhDs often cost nothing out of pocket through assistantships, while self-funded PsyD and EdD programs can exceed $100,000 to $200,000 in total.</li>
  <li>Fully online doctorates are extremely limited, and the APA accredits no fully online psychology doctorate. Verify any online doctorate claim directly with the school before applying.</li>
  <li>You do not need a doctorate to consult with athletes. A master's degree plus CMPC certification reaches paid consulting work in roughly four to six years, about half the time of the doctoral route to licensure.</li>
</ul></div>

<p>I have spent years mapping how sport psychology is taught, and the doctorate is the question readers ask me with the most at stake. A doctorate in sports psychology, which most people mean when they say a PhD in sports psychology, is the longest and most expensive route into this field, and it is also the only one that leads to the psychologist title. The honest picture is more complicated than the brochures suggest: the degree comes in two very different forms, only one of them leads to licensure, and the cheaper, faster route to working with athletes may not be a doctorate at all. This guide separates the paths, names the real programs, and gives you the costs, timelines, and application steps without the sales pitch.</p>

<h2 id="myth-bust">The first fact: no APA-accredited sports psychology PhD exists</h2>

<p>Start here, because every strong guide to this topic starts here and any guide that skips it is not telling you the truth. The American Psychological Association accredits doctoral programs in Clinical, Counseling, and School Psychology, plus a handful of Combined programs. Sports psychology is not on that list. The APA treats it as an applied specialization that psychologists typically add on top of a general doctorate, through supervised experience or continuing education, not as its own accredited doctoral track.</p>

<p>That means no program anywhere can honestly advertise an APA-accredited PhD in sports psychology. What you will actually find are seven real doctoral programs in the United States with a genuine sport or performance concentration, split between two very different paths. Figuring out which path matches your goals matters more than any program's marketing copy, and it is the decision this guide is built around.</p>

<p>One more point before the details: the title sport psychologist is legally protected. Only a person holding a state psychology license can use it. A doctorate in kinesiology or sport science, however strong, does not confer that title. If the title matters to you, the licensure track below is the only route.</p>

<h2 id="two-tracks">Two tracks, one name</h2>

<p>Every doctorate in this field runs on one of two tracks. I have read the program pages, and the split is consistent across all of them.</p>

<p>The <strong>licensure track</strong> is a doctorate in Counseling Psychology or Clinical Psychology with sport-focused coursework or practicum. It leads toward becoming a licensed psychologist through your state board and the EPPP licensing exam, with the legal right to use the title psychologist and to diagnose and treat clients clinically. This is the track for anyone who wants to do clinical work with athletes, including anxiety, depression, and eating disorders alongside performance work.</p>

<p>The <strong>applied and research track</strong> is a doctorate in Kinesiology or Sport and Exercise Psychology. It trains you for academic, research, or consulting roles and toward Certified Mental Performance Consultant (CMPC) status through the Association for Applied Sport Psychology. The CMPC is a certification, not a state license: it certifies competence in mental performance consulting, helping athletes build the mental skills behind consistent execution. It does not authorize clinical treatment or the psychologist title.</p>

<p>Here is the chooser I give readers. If you want to diagnose and treat athletes, take the licensure track. If you want to consult on mental skills, focus, and routines without the psychologist title, take the applied track and add the CMPC. If you want a university faculty job, take the research-heavy PhD on either track, because hiring committees weigh research output and publications above the degree label. Name the job first, then pick the track that feeds it. Our <a href="/degrees/certification/">CMPC certification guide</a> explains the credential in full, and our <a href="/blog/sports-psychology-masters-programs/">master's programs guide</a> covers the shorter route in detail.</p>

<h2 id="programs">Sports psychology PhD programs compared</h2>

<p>Seven US universities run doctoral programs with a real sport or performance concentration. All seven are institutionally accredited. Only one carries APA accreditation, and only because it is a general counseling psychology doctorate rather than a stand-alone sport psychology degree. Verify current details with each school before applying, because programs change requirements, faculty, and funding year to year.</p>

